From: mirx developers
Subject: [PATCH] win32u: Do not clear a reused window handle's object pointer.

free_window_handle asks the server to destroy the window, which frees
its user handle, and then sets client_objects[index] to NULL.  But as
soon as the server has freed the handle, another thread can allocate
the same index (with a new generation) for a new window, menu or other
user object: alloc_user_handle stores the new object's pointer without
user_lock.  If that store comes first, free_window_handle's NULL store
then wipes it, and the new handle is valid on the server but has no
object: every later use of it fails as an invalid handle.

Clear the pointer only if it still points to the window being freed,
as free_user_handle already does for the other object types.

Seen as AppendMenuW failing on a menu that CreatePopupMenu had just
returned (no error set; grab_menu_ptr warns "invalid menu handle"),
while another thread destroyed a window whose index the menu reused.
12 threads that each create and destroy 150 frame windows with menus
hit it on almost every run.

---
--- a/dlls/win32u/window.c
+++ b/dlls/win32u/window.c
@@ -5207,7 +5207,9 @@ static void free_window_handle( HWND hwnd )
         {
             req->handle = wine_server_user_handle( hwnd );
             wine_server_call( req );
-            set_user_handle_ptr( hwnd, NULL );
+            /* The server has freed the handle, so another thread may already
+             * have allocated its index and stored its own object there. */
+            InterlockedCompareExchangePointer( &client_objects[USER_HANDLE_TO_INDEX( hwnd )], NULL, win );
         }
         SERVER_END_REQ;
         user_unlock();
